.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm libotelexport
is the exporter layer shared by the ObservableBSD tools
.Xr bsdinstruments 8
and
.Xr hwstat 8 .
A tool's run loop normalizes probe firings into
.Vt struct oe_event
records, aggregation results into
.Vt struct oe_snapshot
records and timed operations into
.Vt struct oe_span
records, then hands them to an exporter object which ships them to a
destination: a stream or rotating file as text, JSON Lines, collapsed
stacks or OTLP JSON Lines, or an OpenTelemetry collector over OTLP/HTTP
with protobuf or JSON bodies, or over OTLP/gRPC.
The library is a C port of the ObservableBSD Swift
.Dq OTelExport
library.
.Ss Exporter objects
Every exporter is a
.Vt struct oe_exporter
whose only public member,
.Va ops ,
points at a
.Vt struct oe_exporter_ops
method table:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_exporter_ops {
	int	(*start)(struct oe_exporter *);
	int	(*event)(struct oe_exporter *, const struct oe_event *);
	int	(*snapshot)(struct oe_exporter *,
		    const struct oe_snapshot *);
	int	(*flush)(struct oe_exporter *);
	int	(*shutdown)(struct oe_exporter *);
	void	(*free)(struct oe_exporter *);
	int	(*span)(struct oe_exporter *, const struct oe_span *);
};
.Ed
.Pp
Concrete exporters embed
.Vt struct oe_exporter
as their first member.
Callers never invoke the table directly; the macros
.Fn oe_start ,
.Fn oe_event ,
.Fn oe_snapshot ,
.Fn oe_span ,
.Fn oe_flush ,
.Fn oe_shutdown
and
.Fn oe_exporter_free
dispatch through it.
An exporter whose table has no
.Va span
method accepts and ignores spans.
The intended life cycle is: construct, call
.Fn oe_start
once, deliver any number of events and snapshots, optionally call
.Fn oe_flush
at checkpoints, call
.Fn oe_shutdown
to drain, then
.Fn oe_exporter_free
to release the object.
.Fn oe_shutdown
may be called on an exporter that was never started.
.Pp
.Fn oe_event
delivers one probe firing.
All strings and frame arrays in the
.Vt struct oe_event
are borrowed for the duration of the call; exporters copy what they keep.
.Va ts
is the probe's own wall-clock time and becomes the record's
.Ql timeUnixNano ;
.Va observed
is when the consumer read the record
.Pq Ql observedTimeUnixNano ,
and a zero
.Va observed
means the time of encoding.
Events with a
.Dv NULL
or empty
.Va body
are silently ignored by the text, JSONL and OTLP exporters.
.Va severity
is the record's
.Ql severityNumber
in the OpenTelemetry log data model
.Po
.Dv OE_SEV_TRACE
1,
.Dv OE_SEV_DEBUG
5,
.Dv OE_SEV_INFO
9,
.Dv OE_SEV_WARN
13,
.Dv OE_SEV_ERROR
17,
.Dv OE_SEV_FATAL
21, and the values in between
.Pc ;
.Dv OE_SEV_UNSPECIFIED
.Pq 0
and out-of-range values export as INFO.
.Va attrs
and
.Va nattrs
add typed log record attributes after the context attributes.
Both are used by the OTLP exporters; JSONL lines carry the attributes
in their
.Va attributes
object and a specified severity as
.Va severity
.Pq the severityText .
.Fn oe_severity_text
writes the
.Ql severityText
for a severity number: the range's short name
.Pq Ql TRACE , Ql DEBUG , Ql INFO , Ql WARN , Ql ERROR , Ql FATAL
followed by the offset within the range when it is not the first value
.Pq Ql WARN2 ,
and returns
.Fa buf .
.Fn oe_snapshot
delivers one aggregation snapshot; its
.Va kind
is one of
.Dv OE_AGG_COUNT ,
.Dv OE_AGG_SUM ,
.Dv OE_AGG_MIN ,
.Dv OE_AGG_MAX ,
.Dv OE_AGG_AVG ,
.Dv OE_AGG_STDDEV ,
.Dv OE_AGG_QUANTIZE ,
.Dv OE_AGG_LQUANTIZE
or
.Dv OE_AGG_LLQUANTIZE ,
and each
.Vt struct oe_datapoint
carries either a scalar or, when
.Va is_histogram
is set, an array of
.Vt struct oe_bucket
with inclusive upper bounds.
A non-zero
.Va start
is the start of the accumulation window and becomes the
.Ql startTimeUnixNano
of cumulative sums and histograms.
Snapshots with zero points are ignored.
.Pp
.Fn oe_span
delivers one finished span:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_span {
	const char	*name;
	const char	*profile;
	const char	*probe;
	struct timespec	 start, end;
	uint8_t		 trace_id[OE_TRACE_ID_LEN];
	uint8_t		 span_id[OE_SPAN_ID_LEN];
	uint8_t		 parent_span_id[OE_SPAN_ID_LEN];
	enum oe_span_kind kind;
	enum oe_status_code status;
	const char	*status_message;
	pid_t		 pid;
	const char	*execname;
	const struct oe_kv *attrs;
	size_t		 nattrs;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Times are wall clock; an all-zero
.Va parent_span_id
marks a root span.
.Va kind
.Pq Dv OE_SPAN_INTERNAL , OE_SPAN_SERVER , OE_SPAN_CLIENT , OE_SPAN_PRODUCER , OE_SPAN_CONSUMER
and
.Va status
.Pq Dv OE_STATUS_UNSET , OE_STATUS_OK , OE_STATUS_ERROR
are numbered as in the OTLP trace protocol.
.Va attrs
is an array of typed attributes:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_kv {
	const char	*key;
	enum oe_value_type type;	/* OE_VAL_STRING, _INT, _DOUBLE, _BOOL */
	const char	*s;
	int64_t		 i;		/* integer or boolean */
	double		 d;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Like events, spans are borrowed for the duration of the call.
.Ss Semantic conventions
Both
.Vt struct oe_event
and
.Vt struct oe_span
carry a
.Vt struct oe_context
describing who produced the record:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_context {
	unsigned	 flags;
	pid_t		 ppid;
	int64_t		 tid;
	uid_t		 uid, ruid;
	gid_t		 gid, rgid;
	int		 cpu;
	int		 jid;
	const char	*jail_name;
};
.Ed
.Pp
Every format exports it under the OpenTelemetry semantic-convention
names, from a single definition so log records, spans and JSON Lines
agree:
.Va pid
as
.Ql process.pid
.Pq when non-zero, or when Dv OE_CTX_PID says it is known ,
.Ql process.parent_pid
.Pq Dv OE_CTX_PPID ,
the event's or span's
.Va execname
as
.Ql process.executable.name ,
.Ql process.user.id
and
.Ql process.real_user.id
.Pq Dv OE_CTX_UID , OE_CTX_RUID ,
.Ql process.group.id
and
.Ql process.real_group.id
.Pq Dv OE_CTX_GID , OE_CTX_RGID ,
.Ql thread.id
.Pq Dv OE_CTX_TID
and
.Ql cpu.logical_number
.Pq Dv OE_CTX_CPU ,
all as integers.
A jail
.Pq Dv OE_CTX_JAIL with a non-zero Va jid
is reported the way the conventions describe a container:
.Ql container.id
is the jail id,
.Ql container.name
its name when known, and
.Ql container.runtime.name
is
.Ql jail ;
the host itself is not a container.
Metric data point attributes with one of these integer names are
emitted as integers when their value is a decimal number, so a pid has
the same type in a metric as in a log record.
.Ss Output sinks
The file-writing exporters write through a
.Vt struct oe_sink ,
configured by:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_sink_config {
	const char	*path;
	FILE		*fp;
	uint64_t	 rotate_size;
	time_t		 rotate_interval;
	unsigned	 rotate_keep;
	int		 durable;
};
.Ed
.Pp
With a
.Dv NULL
.Va path
or
.Ql -
the sink wraps the borrowed stream
.Va fp
.Pq standard output when Dv NULL ,
which is never closed, rotated or synced to disk.
Otherwise
.Fn oe_sink_open
opens or creates
.Va path
for appending; it must be a regular file.
A file rotates before a record that would take it past
.Va rotate_size
bytes, and before the first record written
.Va rotate_interval
seconds after it was opened: the file becomes
.Pa path.1 ,
older files shift up by one, and only
.Va rotate_keep
.Pq default 7, at most 999
rotated files are kept.
Records are written whole, so rotation never splits a record; a record
larger than the limit goes out on its own.
With
.Va durable ,
every flush, rotation and close
.Xr fsync 2 Ns s
the file, and rotation also syncs the directory.
.Pp
.Fn oe_sink_write
writes one record,
.Fn oe_sink_flush
flushes the stream
.Pq and fsyncs it when durable ,
and
.Fn oe_sink_close
flushes and releases the sink, closing the file it owns.
A writer that bypasses the sink, such as libdtrace writing text output
to
.Fn oe_sink_stream ,
calls
.Fn oe_sink_poll
periodically: it flushes, takes the file's size from the file system
and rotates when a limit has been reached.
.Fn oe_sink_stream
may return a different stream after a rotation.
.Fn oe_sink_path
returns the path, or
.Dv NULL
for a stream sink.
The first failure
.Pq for example Er ENOSPC or Er EFBIG
is reported once on standard error with the path and the system error,
and is sticky: every later call fails without writing.
.Ss Stream and file exporters
The
.Vt FILE
pointer passed to
.Fn oe_text_new ,
.Fn oe_jsonl_new
and
.Fn oe_collapsed_new
is borrowed and is never closed by the library; the
.Fn *_sink_new
variants write through a borrowed
.Vt struct oe_sink
instead, so their output can go to a rotating, durable file.
Write failures are returned by the call that hit them.
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Fn oe_text_new , Fn oe_text_sink_new
Line-oriented output.
Prints each event's
.Va body
on its own line, followed by kernel stack frames indented under a
.Ql [k]
marker and user frames under
.Ql [u] .
Snapshots print as a tabular dump headed by the aggregation name, kind and
profile, one line per data point, with non-empty histogram buckets listed
beneath.
Spans print as one line:
.Ql span Ar name duration Ns Li us
followed by
.Ql error
and its message when in error, and the trace, span and parent ids.
.Fn oe_flush
and
.Fn oe_shutdown
flush the sink.
.It Fn oe_jsonl_new , Fn oe_jsonl_sink_new , Fn oe_jsonl_resource_new
One JSON object per event on its own line, suitable for piping into
jq, Loki, Vector, Splunk or fluentd.
Each object carries
.Va time
.Pq the probe time in ISO 8601, see Fn oe_iso8601 ,
.Va observed
.Pq when set ,
.Va profile ,
.Va probe
.Pq when non-empty ,
.Va body ,
and, when present,
.Va stack
and
.Va ustack
arrays of formatted frames.
Events and spans carry the context attributes of
.Sx Semantic conventions
in an
.Va attributes
object.
Spans are objects with
.Ql \&"type\&":\&"span\&"
carrying
.Va time
.Pq the start ,
.Va profile ,
.Va name ,
.Va trace_id ,
.Va span_id ,
.Va parent_span_id
.Pq child spans ,
.Va end ,
.Va duration_ns ,
.Va kind ,
.Va status ,
.Va status_message
and
.Va probe
when known, and an
.Va attributes
object holding the context attributes and then the span's own;
non-finite doubles become
.Dv null .
Events keep their original shape, without a
.Va type
member.
The
.Fa profile
argument is copied at construction; a
.Dv NULL
profile is treated as the empty string.
Snapshots become objects with
.Ql \&"type\&":\&"metric\&"
carrying
.Va time ,
.Va start
.Pq the start of a cumulative window, when set ,
.Va profile ,
.Va name
.Pq the kind for an anonymous aggregation ,
.Va kind
and a
.Va points
array whose entries hold the key dimensions in
.Va attributes
.Pq string values
and either a
.Va value
or, for the quantize family, a
.Va buckets
array of
.Va le
.Pq inclusive upper bound
and
.Va count
pairs; snapshots without data points are skipped.
.Fn oe_jsonl_resource_new
also names a resource in every line: a
.Va resource
object holding copies of the
.Fa n
attributes of
.Fa resource ,
as
.Xr dtrace 1
writes for each HyperTrace guest.
.It Fn oe_collapsed_new , Fn oe_collapsed_sink_new
Folded stacks for flame graph generation in the format read by
.Pa flamegraph.pl ,
speedscope and pprof:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
execname;module`func;module`func 42
.Ed
.Pp
Stacks are accumulated in memory during the run and written, sorted by
stack path, at
.Fn oe_shutdown .
DTrace reports frames leaf-first; the exporter reverses them so the
process name is the root frame.
When an event carries both kernel and user stacks the kernel stack is
placed first and separated from the user stack by a
.Ql --
frame.
Events without any stack are ignored; events with a stack but an empty
body are folded.
Spans are ignored.
.Fn oe_flush
is a no-op.
.It Fn oe_otlpjson_new
OTLP JSON Lines: one
.Vt ExportLogsServiceRequest ,
.Vt ExportMetricsServiceRequest
or
.Vt ExportTraceServiceRequest
JSON object per line, the format written by the OpenTelemetry
Collector's file exporter and read by its
.Ql otlpjsonfile
receiver.
The requests are built by the same encoder as the OTLP/HTTP exporter's
request bodies and are byte-for-byte identical to them.
It is configured by:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_otlpjson_config {
	struct oe_sink	*sink;
	const char	*profile;
	const struct oe_resource *resource;
	int		 batch_size;
	const char	*scope_name;
};
.Ed
.Pp
.Va resource
and
.Va scope_name
are as for the OTLP/HTTP exporter below.
Log records and spans are written once
.Va batch_size
.Pq default 200
of a kind are pending; snapshots and any pending records are written by
.Fn oe_flush
and
.Fn oe_shutdown ,
which also flush the sink.
The exporter runs on the caller's thread.
.El
.Ss OTLP exporter
.Fn oe_otlp_new
creates an exporter that batches events as OpenTelemetry
.Vt LogRecord
objects, aggregation snapshots as OTLP metrics and spans as OTLP spans,
and sends each batch to a collector with the configured
.Va protocol :
.Bl -tag -width "http/protobuf"
.It Ql http/protobuf
OTLP/HTTP with binary protobuf bodies
.Pq Ql Content-Type: application/x-protobuf ,
POSTed to
.Pa /v1/logs ,
.Pa /v1/metrics
and
.Pa /v1/traces
relative to the endpoint.
.It Ql grpc
OTLP/gRPC: a unary call of the
.Ql Export
method of
.Ql opentelemetry.proto.collector.logs.v1.LogsService ,
.Ql ...metrics.v1.MetricsService
or
.Ql ...trace.v1.TraceService
over HTTP/2
.Pq with the nghttp2 library ,
in cleartext with prior knowledge for an
.Ql http
endpoint and over TLS with ALPN
.Ql h2
for an
.Ql https
endpoint.
The endpoint's path is not used.
.It Ql http/json
OTLP/HTTP with the protobuf JSON encoding
.Pq Ql Content-Type: application/json ,
to the same paths as
.Ql http/protobuf .
.El
.Pp
All three carry the same requests: the protobuf transports transcode the
JSON request the exporter batches and spools
.Pq the format Fn oe_otlpjson_new writes
into the binary
.Vt Export*ServiceRequest
with upb, using the
.Pa opentelemetry-proto
1.10.0 definitions compiled into the library.
It is configured by a
.Vt struct oe_otlp_config :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_otlp_config {
	const char	*endpoint;
	const char	*profile;
	const struct oe_resource *resource;
	int		 batch_size;
	double		 flush_interval;
	int		 max_retries;
	double		 timeout;
	const struct oe_attr *headers;
	size_t		 nheaders;
	const char	*compression;
	const char	*spool_path;
	uint64_t	 spool_max;
	const char	*scope_name;
	int		 spool_optional;
	const char	*protocol;
};
.Ed
.Bl -tag -width "flush_interval"
.It Va endpoint
Base URL of the collector, for example
.Ql http://localhost:4318
.Pq OTLP/HTTP
or
.Ql http://localhost:4317
.Pq OTLP/gRPC .
Only the
.Ql http
and
.Ql https
schemes are accepted; an optional port and base path may follow the host,
and bracketed IPv6 literals are supported.
A trailing slash on the base path is dropped.
For
.Ql https
the peer certificate is verified against the system trust store and the
host name is checked.
.It Va profile
Profile name recorded in each log record's
.Ql <scope>.profile
attribute and in metric names.
.Dv NULL
is treated as the empty string.
.It Va resource
Pointer to a
.Vt struct oe_resource
describing the OTel resource attached to every batch.
Must not be
.Dv NULL .
Its
.Va service_version
is the scope version, the
.Ql telemetry.sdk.version
attribute and the HTTP
.Ql User-Agent
.Pq Ql OTel-OTLP-Exporter-C/<version> .
The library emits
.Ql service.name ,
.Ql service.version ,
.Ql service.instance.id ,
.Ql host.name ,
.Ql host.id ,
.Ql host.arch ,
.Ql os.type
.Pq from Va os_name ,
.Ql os.name
.Pq from Va os_full_name ,
.Ql os.version ,
.Ql os.description ,
.Ql telemetry.sdk.name
.Pq Ql observablebsd ,
.Ql telemetry.sdk.language
.Pq Ql c
and
.Ql telemetry.sdk.version ,
omitting
.Dv NULL
members, then the
.Va custom
array of
.Vt struct oe_attr .
A custom attribute replaces a built-in one of the same key
.Pq so Ev OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES can correct a detected value without producing a duplicate key ,
except
.Ql service.name ,
which the tool resolves itself; of duplicate custom keys the last wins.
.It Va scope_name
Instrumentation scope name, normally the tool
.Pq for example Ql bsdinstruments .
It prefixes the tool's own attributes and metric names below.
.Dv NULL
falls back to the resource's
.Va service_name .
.It Va batch_size
Number of log records that triggers an immediate batch post.
0 selects the default of 200.
.It Va flush_interval
Seconds between time-based flushes performed by the sender thread.
0 selects the default of 0.5.
.It Va max_retries
Number of additional attempts after a failed POST.
A negative value selects the default of 2.
.It Va timeout
Connect, send and receive timeout in seconds for each HTTP request; for
OTLP/gRPC the deadline of the whole call, also sent as
.Ql grpc-timeout .
0 selects the default of 10.
.It Va headers , Va nheaders
Extra HTTP request headers, sent verbatim on every POST; for OTLP/gRPC
they are sent as call metadata with lowercase names, and names the gRPC
framing owns
.Pq for example Ql content-type , Ql te , Ql grpc-timeout
are skipped.
The array is copied at construction.
.It Va compression
Request bodies are gzip-compressed with
.Ql Content-Encoding: gzip
.Pq OTLP/gRPC: the message, with Ql grpc-encoding: gzip
unless this is the string
.Ql none
.Pq compared case-insensitively .
.Dv NULL
selects gzip.
.It Va spool_path
When non-NULL, requests that still fail after the retries are spooled to
this file instead of being lost; see
.Sx Spooling .
The file is created
.Pq mode 0600
or adopted at construction, so an unusable path fails there.
.It Va spool_max
Spool size limit in bytes; 0 selects 64 MiB.
.It Va protocol
.Ql http/protobuf ,
.Ql grpc
or
.Ql http/json ;
anything else fails construction.
.Dv NULL
selects
.Ql http/json ,
so programs that build their configuration without
.Fn oe_otlp_config_env
keep their behavior;
.Fn oe_otlp_config_env
always sets the resolved protocol, by default
.Ql http/protobuf .
.It Va spool_optional
When non-zero, a spool that cannot be opened, or that another exporter
holds, is reported on standard error and left unused instead of
failing construction.
.Fn oe_config_load
sets it for the system spool of
.Xr otel.conf 5 .
.El
.Pp
.Fn oe_start
launches a dedicated sender thread, with every signal blocked so that
the application's signal handling is unaffected and its
.Dv SIGINT
or
.Dv SIGTERM
reaches the application's own threads; sockets are opened with
.Dv SO_NOSIGPIPE ,
so a collector that drops the connection never raises
.Dv SIGPIPE .
Until
.Fn oe_start
is called nothing is posted
except by an explicit
.Fn oe_flush
or
.Fn oe_shutdown ,
which post synchronously on the calling thread.
.Fn oe_event
appends a record to the current batch; when the batch reaches
.Va batch_size
the envelope is built and queued for the sender.
.Fn oe_snapshot
appends a metric fragment which rides the next time-based flush.
Count and sum aggregations become monotonic cumulative
.Vt Sum
metrics, min/max/avg/stddev become
.Vt Gauge
metrics, and the quantize family become cumulative
.Vt Histogram
metrics whose bucket bounds are sorted ascending; histogram sums are
omitted because power-of-two bucket bounds cannot recover them.
Metric names are
.Ql <scope>.<profile>.<name> ,
falling back to the aggregation kind when the snapshot is anonymous.
Log records carry
.Ql <scope>.profile
and
.Ql <scope>.probe
attributes, the context attributes described in
.Sx Semantic conventions ,
then the event's own attributes, and the event's severity
.Pq INFO when unspecified .
Spans carry the same, followed by their own attributes.
.Pp
Failed requests are retried with exponential backoff starting at 100 ms
plus up to 50% jitter, as recommended by the OTLP specification.
Transport errors are retryable for every protocol.
Over OTLP/HTTP, status 429, 502, 503 and 504 are retryable; a
.Ql Retry-After
header on a retryable response adds its delay to the backoff.
Any other non-2xx status is reported once, with the message of a
.Vt google.rpc.Status
body for
.Ql http/protobuf ,
and the batch is discarded.
Over OTLP/gRPC, the status is the
.Ql grpc-status
of the trailers or of a Trailers-Only response, or is mapped from a
non-200 HTTP status or a stream reset as gRPC defines;
.Dv CANCELLED ,
.Dv DEADLINE_EXCEEDED ,
.Dv ABORTED ,
.Dv OUT_OF_RANGE ,
.Dv UNAVAILABLE
and
.Dv DATA_LOSS
are retryable,
.Dv RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED
only when
.Ql grpc-status-details-bin
carries a
.Vt google.rpc.RetryInfo ,
whose delay is added to the backoff, and every other status is reported
once with its
.Ql grpc-message
and the batch is discarded.
A successful response whose
.Vt partial_success
.Pq JSON Ql partialSuccess
reports rejected log records, data points or spans, or an error message,
is logged to standard error but not retried.
Each request uses a fresh connection
.Pq OTLP/HTTP: Ql Connection: close .
.Pp
.Fn oe_shutdown
stops the sender thread, waits for queued posts to complete, then drains
whatever is still batched synchronously.
.Ss Spooling
Each spool belongs to one exporter: construction takes an exclusive,
non-blocking
.Xr flock 2
lock on a sibling file
.Pa <spool>.lock ,
kept until
.Fn oe_exporter_free .
A spool another exporter holds fails construction
.Pq or is skipped with Va spool_optional ,
because replay rewrites the file and two writers would lose each other's
batches.
.Pp
With a spool configured, a request that fails with a transport error or
a retryable status after
.Va max_retries
retries is appended to the spool as one OTLP JSON line, whatever the
protocol, in the same format
.Fn oe_otlpjson_new
writes, and the file is fsynced.
After the next request the collector accepts, and every five seconds
while the sender thread is idle, the spool is replayed oldest first;
lines the collector accepts or rejects as malformed are removed, the
rest stay for the next attempt.
The remainder is rewritten through a temporary file and
.Xr rename 2 ,
so a crash leaves either the old or the new spool.
A spool left by an earlier run is replayed the same way.
Lines that are not OTLP requests and an incomplete final line are
dropped with a warning.
A request that would push the spool past
.Va spool_max
is dropped; the number of batches dropped is reported once the spool
drains.
Requests rejected with a non-retryable status are never spooled.
.Ss Drop accounting
.Fn oe_report_drops
adds
.Fa count
kernel-side record drops
.Pq for example DTrace buffer drops
to a pending counter on an OTLP exporter.
The accumulated value is spliced into the first log record of the next
batch as an integer attribute named
.Ql <scope>.drops
and the counter is reset.
If a flush finds no log records to carry the attribute
.Pq for example an aggregation-only profile
the drops are re-credited and ride a later batch rather than being lost.
The function is a no-op for the standard-output exporters and for a
.Dv NULL
exporter.
.Ss Environment
.Fn oe_env_load
fills a
.Vt struct oe_env
from the OpenTelemetry standard environment variables listed in
.Sx ENVIRONMENT .
String members point directly into the environment and must not be
freed; the
.Va resource_attrs
and
.Va headers
arrays are allocated and released by
.Fn oe_env_free ,
which also zeroes the structure.
Comma-separated
.Ql key=value
lists
.Pq the W3C Baggage format the specification uses
are parsed into
.Vt struct oe_attr
arrays: blanks around keys and values are trimmed, empty entries are
skipped and values are percent-decoded.
As the specification asks, a malformed list
.Pq an entry without Ql = , an empty key or a bad percent escape
is reported on standard error and discarded as a whole, never applied in
part; header lists are also discarded when a name is not an HTTP token
or a value contains a control character, so the environment cannot
inject request lines.
A timeout that is not a non-negative number of milliseconds and a
compression other than
.Ql gzip
or
.Ql none
are reported and ignored; empty variables count as unset.
.Fn oe_env_load
only parses the environment.
.Ss Configuration layers
.Fn oe_config_load
is the one configuration path of every exporting tool
.Po
.Xr dtrace 1 ,
.Xr bsdinstruments 8 ,
.Xr hwstat 8 ,
.Xr otelpfd 8 ,
.Xr otelauditd 8
.Pc .
It resolves the exporter settings across all layers, highest first:
command line, the
.Ev OTEL_*
environment
.Pq Fn oe_env_load ,
the system configuration
.Xr otel.conf 5
when
.Va req->system
is set and the file's
.Ic enable
key is true, and the built-in defaults:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_config_request {
	const char	*scope;		/* tool; names the spool file */
	const char	*endpoint;	/* command line, or NULL */
	const char	*spool;		/* command line, or NULL */
	uint64_t	 spool_max;	/* command line, or 0 */
	int		 system;	/* read otel.conf(5) */
	const char	*protocol;	/* command line, or NULL */
};
.Ed
.Pp
On success
.Va env->protocol
is always set
.Pq Dv OE_DEFAULT_PROTOCOL , Ql http/protobuf , at the bottom
and so is
.Va env->endpoint
.Pq Dv OE_DEFAULT_ENDPOINT , Ql http://localhost:4318 , at the bottom, or Dv OE_DEFAULT_GRPC_ENDPOINT , Ql http://localhost:4317 , for Ql grpc ;
an unknown command line protocol is an error;
environment resource attributes and headers win over system ones with
the same name, and credentials from the
.Xr otel.conf 5
headers file are added only when the resolved endpoint is the system
endpoint.
.Va env->spool_path
is the command line spool, else the system spool
.Pa <spool.directory>/<scope>.spool
when this process may write that directory, else
.Dv NULL ;
.Va env->spool_max
is the command line bound, else
.Ic spool.max_bytes .
.Va env
owns the loaded system layer; release it with
.Fn oe_env_free .
An invalid
.Xr otel.conf 5
or an allocation failure returns \-1 with a description in
.Fa err
and leaves
.Fa env
empty.
.Pp
.Fn oe_resource_init
builds a tool's resource from the detected host
.Pq Fn oe_host_detect ,
the service name
.Pq Ev OTEL_SERVICE_NAME , else a Ql service.name resource attribute, else Fa service ,
.Fa version ,
and the remaining resource attributes as custom attributes.
.Fn oe_otlp_config_env
copies the resolved endpoint, protocol, timeout, headers, compression and
spool settings into a
.Vt struct oe_otlp_config ,
leaving its other members alone.
.Ss Host detection
.Fn oe_host_detect
fills a
.Vt struct oe_host_info
with the values of the OpenTelemetry resource conventions:
.Va host_name ,
.Va host_id
.Pq Va kern.hostuuid ,
.Va host_arch
.Pq Va hw.machine_arch in the conventions' spelling, see below ,
.Va os_type
.Pq Ql freebsd ,
.Va os_name
and
.Va os_version
.Pq Xr uname 3 ,
.Va os_description
.Pq system name, release and Va kern.ident, for example Ql FreeBSD 16.0-CURRENT GENERIC
and a fresh random version 4 UUID in
.Va instance_id
for
.Ql service.instance.id .
Unknown values are empty strings.
.Fn oe_resource_host
points the host and operating system members of a
.Vt struct oe_resource
.Pq and Va service_instance_id
at those values, leaving empty ones
.Dv NULL .
.Fn oe_host_arch_semconv
maps a
.Va hw.machine_arch
value to the conventions' spelling
.Pq amd64, arm64 for aarch64, x86 for i386, ppc64, ppc32, arm32 ;
architectures they do not list keep the system's name.
.Pp
.Fn oe_host_name ,
.Fn oe_host_arch
and
.Fn oe_os_version
fill caller-provided buffers with the values customarily placed in the
.Va host_name ,
.Va host_arch
and
.Va os_version
resource members, using
.Xr gethostname 3
.Pq falling back to Ql localhost
and the
.Va machine
and
.Va release
members of
.Xr uname 3
.Pq falling back to the empty string .
.Ss String buffer and formatting helpers
.Vt struct oe_buf
is a growable, always NUL-terminated string buffer used by the exporters
to build output and exported for the benefit of tools that render event
bodies.
.Fn oe_buf_init
zeroes the buffer,
.Fn oe_buf_free
releases its storage and re-initializes it, and
.Fn oe_buf_append ,
.Fn oe_buf_appendstr
and
.Fn oe_buf_appendf
append raw bytes, a C string, or
.Xr printf 3 Ns -formatted
text.
.Fn oe_buf_appendjson
appends
.Fa s
with RFC 8259 string escaping and no surrounding quotes: the quotation
mark, the backslash and the control characters below 0x20 are escaped
.Po
.Ql \en ,
.Ql \et
and the other short forms where JSON has them,
.Ql \eu00xx
otherwise
.Pc ,
and well-formed UTF-8 is copied unchanged.
Since DTrace strings are arbitrary bytes, any byte that does not begin a
well-formed UTF-8 sequence (a stray continuation byte, a truncated,
overlong or surrogate sequence, or a code point above U+10FFFF) is
replaced by U+FFFD, so the output is always valid UTF-8 JSON.
Every exporter writes its strings this way.
A
.Dv NULL
argument is a no-op.
Once any operation fails the buffer's
.Va error
flag is set and every further append fails, so a sequence of appends can
be checked once at the end.
.Pp
.Fn oe_env_load
is the single place the tools consult for these defaults, so a
lower-precedence configuration source
.Pq for example a system-wide file
belongs there, filling only members the environment left unset.
The resulting precedence is: command line, then
.Ev OTEL_*
environment, then such a configuration layer, then the built-in default.
.Pp
.Fn oe_frame_format
renders a
.Vt struct oe_frame
as
.Ql module`symbol+0xoff
.Pq or without the offset when Va has_offset is clear ,
as the bare symbol when no module is known, or as
.Ql 0x
followed by the 16-digit hexadecimal address when no symbol is known.
.Fn oe_iso8601
formats a
.Vt struct timespec
as
.Ql YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.mmmZ ,
always in UTC so that timestamps sort lexically across hosts.
.Ss Trace and span ids
.Fn oe_id_random
fills
.Fa len
bytes with a random id.
.Fn oe_id_derive
derives a stable id from a 16-byte secret
.Fa salt ,
a short namespace string
.Fa ns
and up to eight integer keys: the first
.Fa len
bytes of the SHA-256 digest of the salt, the namespace NUL-padded to 32
bytes and the keys as little-endian 64-bit integers.
Two producers that know the same keys therefore name the same span
without coordinating, while a different salt gives unrelated ids.
Both functions never produce the all-zero id, which W3C Trace Context
reserves as invalid; a zero result has its last byte set to 1.
.Fn oe_id_hex
writes
.Fa 2*len
lowercase hexadecimal digits and a NUL to
.Fa buf ,
the form OTLP JSON uses for ids, and
.Fn oe_id_is_zero
tests for the invalid id.
.Ss Thread safety
The OTLP exporter serializes its batch state with an internal mutex and
may be fed from multiple threads;
.Fn oe_report_drops
is likewise safe to call from any thread, including a DTrace drop
handler running concurrently with the consumer loop.
.Fn oe_start ,
.Fn oe_shutdown
and
.Fn oe_exporter_free
must not race one another or the delivery calls.
The text, JSONL and collapsed exporters, the
.Vt struct oe_buf
helpers and
.Fn oe_env_load
perform no locking of their own; callers sharing one of these objects
between threads must serialize access.
Diagnostics from the OTLP exporter are written to standard error.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
The constructors return a pointer to a new exporter, or
.Dv NULL
on failure.
.Fn oe_start ,
.Fn oe_event ,
.Fn oe_snapshot ,
.Fn oe_flush ,
.Fn oe_shutdown
and the
.Fn oe_buf_append*
family return zero on success and \-1 on failure.
The library does not set
.Va errno ;
where a cause is known it is reported on standard error.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Fn oe_env_load
reads the following variables; the corresponding
.Vt struct oe_env
member is shown together with the value it takes when the variable is
unset.
.Bl -tag -width "OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_COMPRESSION"
.It Ev OTEL_SERVICE_NAME
.Va service_name ;
.Dv NULL
if unset.
The tools then fall back to their own program name.
.It Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT
.Va endpoint ;
.Dv NULL
if unset.
.It Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS
.Va headers
and
.Va nheaders ,
parsed from a comma-separated
.Ql Name=value
list; empty if unset.
.It Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_COMPRESSION
.Va compression ;
.Dv NULL
if unset, which the OTLP exporter treats as gzip.
.It Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_TIMEOUT
.Va timeout_ms ,
in milliseconds; \-1 if unset.
.It Ev OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_PROTOCOL
.Va protocol :
.Ql grpc ,
.Ql http/protobuf
or
.Ql http/json ;
another value is reported and ignored.
.Dv NULL
if unset;
.Fn oe_config_load
then takes
.Xr otel.conf 5
.Ic protocol ,
else
.Ql http/protobuf .
.It Ev OTEL_RESOURCE_ATTRIBUTES
.Va resource_attrs
and
.Va nresource_attrs ,
parsed from a comma-separated
.Ql key=value
list; empty if unset.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Build an OTLP exporter from the environment, as the tools do:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
struct oe_env env;
struct oe_host_info hi;
struct oe_resource res;
struct oe_otlp_config cfg;
struct oe_exporter *e;

oe_env_load(&env);
memset(&res, 0, sizeof(res));
oe_host_detect(&hi);
oe_resource_host(&res, &hi);
res.service_name = env.service_name != NULL ? env.service_name :
    "mytool";
res.service_version = "1.0";
res.custom = env.resource_attrs;
res.ncustom = env.nresource_attrs;

memset(&cfg, 0, sizeof(cfg));
cfg.endpoint = env.endpoint != NULL ? env.endpoint :
    "http://localhost:4318";
cfg.profile = "syscalls";
cfg.resource = &res;
cfg.scope_name = "mytool";
cfg.max_retries = -1;
cfg.timeout = env.timeout_ms > 0 ? env.timeout_ms / 1000.0 : 0;
cfg.headers = env.headers;
cfg.nheaders = env.nheaders;
cfg.compression = env.compression;

e = oe_otlp_new(&cfg);
if (e == NULL || oe_start(e) != 0)
	errx(1, "cannot create exporter");
/* ... oe_event(e, &ev); oe_snapshot(e, &sn); ... */
oe_shutdown(e);
oe_exporter_free(e);
oe_env_free(&env);
.Ed
.Sh ERRORS
Constructors fail on memory exhaustion.
.Fn oe_otlp_new
additionally fails when
.Va endpoint
is not a well-formed
.Ql http
or
.Ql https
URL.
.Fn oe_start
fails if the sender thread cannot be created.
.Fn oe_event
and
.Fn oe_snapshot
fail only on memory exhaustion; network failures are handled by the
sender thread and never surface through the delivery calls.
.Fn oe_shutdown
of the collapsed exporter fails if the sort buffer cannot be allocated.
The stream and file exporters' delivery, flush and shutdown calls also
fail when the sink cannot be written; the sink's own calls fail on any
write, sync, rotation or close error.
.Fn oe_sink_open
fails when the file cannot be opened or created, is not a regular file,
the path leaves no room for the rotation suffix, or
.Va rotate_keep
exceeds 999.
With a spool,
.Fn oe_otlp_new
also fails when the spool cannot be opened or created or is not a
regular file.
